# AgentGateway — Build Checklist

Derived from [ROADMAP.md](ROADMAP.md). Design rationale lives in [WHITEPAPER.md](WHITEPAPER.md).

## Phase 0 — Repo Scaffold

- [x] Create the monorepo structure
- [x] Get `docker-compose up` to succeed
- [x] Apply the Prisma migration
- [x] Confirm `/health` returns 200

## Phase 1 — Foundation & Infrastructure Setup

- [ ] Scaffold the repo with a strict TypeScript config
- [ ] Configure ESLint and Prettier
- [ ] Write `docker-compose.yml` bringing up Postgres + Redis + the app in one command
- [ ] Build the environment config module
- [ ] Commit `.env.example` and keep secrets out of version control
- [ ] Implement the `/health` endpoint checking DB and Redis connectivity
- [ ] Wire in Razorpay test-mode keys
- [ ] Write a manual script that creates one test Order and confirms the SDK round-trips correctly

**Validation:**

- [ ] `docker-compose up` succeeds from a clean clone
- [ ] `/health` returns 200
- [ ] A test Order appears in the Razorpay test-mode dashboard

## Phase 2 — Database Layer, Policy Engine & Razorpay Core

- [x] Write migrations for all eight tables, with indexes and constraints as specified
- [x] Implement `PolicyEngine.checkSpendCap()` using the row-locked transactional check from §3.5
- [x] Write unit tests covering the concurrent-request race explicitly (two simultaneous calls, assert only one succeeds)
- [ ] Implement `RazorpayClient.createOrder()`, `.createPaymentLink()`, `.capturePayment()` against test-mode credentials
- [x] Build the webhook listener with raw-body capture + HMAC verification (§3.4)
- [x] Store every webhook event in `webhook_events` regardless of validity — log and reject invalid ones rather than silently dropping them

**Validation:**

- [x] A concurrency test proves the spend-cap check cannot be raced
- [ ] A manually-triggered Razorpay test webhook is received, verified, and persisted correctly
- [x] A deliberately tampered signature is rejected and logged

## Phase 3 — Protocol Adapters & Cryptographic Engine

- [x] Build `x402Adapter` to issue the `402` challenge
- [x] Validate retried x402 requests against the original reference and enforce one-time redemption
- [x] Build `ap2Adapter` to canonicalize and verify `IntentMandate` signatures
- [x] Enforce nonce uniqueness in `ap2Adapter` across Redis and the DB
- [x] Map validated AP2 mandates to `NormalizedPaymentRequest`
- [x] Build `fallbackAdapter` with Payment Link generation + the human-confirmation webhook path
- [x] Build the protocol router that inspects request shape/headers and dispatches to the correct adapter, defaulting to `fallbackAdapter` for anything unrecognized

**Validation:**

- [x] A tampered AP2 signature is rejected pre-database
- [x] A replayed nonce is rejected by Redis on the fast path
- [x] In a forced-Redis-outage test, a replayed nonce is rejected by the Postgres unique constraint as a fallback
- [ ] An x402 flow completes end-to-end against test-mode settlement

## Phase 4 — Reference AI Agent Client

- [ ] Build a CLI agent invocable as `--protocol=x402` and `--protocol=ap2`, both purchasing the same test cart against the local gateway
- [ ] Have the agent generate its own Ed25519 keypair for the AP2 run
- [ ] Write a setup script that registers the agent's public key (simulating merchant-side agent onboarding)
- [ ] Log the full request/response trace to a JSON file for the Phase 5 tester panel and the demo video

**Validation:**

- [ ] Both protocol runs, against the identical cart and identical merchant, terminate in the same `razorpay_orders` row shape underneath

## Phase 5 — Merchant Dashboard & Interactive Protocol Tester

- [ ] Build the policy console for spend caps, blocked categories, and enabled protocols per merchant, writing to `/v1/merchant/policy`
- [ ] Build the live transaction/audit feed over SSE, showing protocol origin, status, and a link into each request's full audit trail
- [ ] Build the protocol tester panel: paste or trigger a raw x402/AP2 request from the browser and render the adapter's validate → normalize → settle → receipt steps side by side in real time
- [ ] Build the agent management view with a one-click revoke button (`POST /v1/merchant/agents/:id/revoke`)
- [ ] Demonstrate that a revoked agent's next request is rejected immediately

**Validation:**

- [ ] Revoking an agent mid-session blocks its next request
- [ ] The audit trail for a rejected mandate is fully readable by a non-technical viewer without needing to read logs

## Phase 6 — End-to-End Integration, Chaos Testing & Submission Prep

- [ ] Script and record chaos scenario 1: replay a captured AP2 mandate — must be rejected
- [ ] Script and record chaos scenario 2: fire 20 concurrent spend-cap-adjacent requests from the same agent — exactly the correct number succeed, verified against the cap
- [ ] Script and record chaos scenario 3: deliver a webhook with a tampered signature — rejected and logged, no state change
- [ ] Script and record chaos scenario 4: deliver the same legitimate webhook twice — processed once
- [ ] Write a load/latency profiling script hitting `/v1/ap2/mandates` at increasing concurrency, reporting p50/p95 latency
- [ ] Write the README with the architecture diagram, setup instructions, and an explicit "known limitations" section (§5.3)
- [ ] Write the 5-minute demo video script: (1) the problem in 30 seconds, (2) the trust-boundary rule stated on camera, (3) both protocol flows completing live, (4) the spend-cap breach handled gracefully on camera, (5) the audit trail for that exact failure shown in the dashboard

**Validation:**

- [ ] Every chaos scenario has a corresponding, visible entry in the audit trail
- [ ] The video shows one real failure end-to-end, not just a success path

## Notes

- [ ] (add blockers / decisions here as you go)

**Phase 2 — left unchecked**

- `RazorpayClient.createOrder()/.createPaymentLink()/.capturePayment()` — implemented and
  unit-tested for exact request shape, but NOT verified against live test-mode
  credentials: `.env` still holds placeholder `rzp_test_xxxx` keys. Fill in real keys and
  run `npm run razorpay:smoke --workspace=gateway`, then re-run
  `npm run webhook:test --workspace=gateway` (it creates a genuine Order when the keys
  are real).
- "A manually-triggered Razorpay test webhook is received, verified, and persisted
  correctly" — verified only with a **locally-signed** Razorpay-shaped delivery, not one
  sent from the Razorpay dashboard's webhook test-send. That needs real credentials plus
  a publicly reachable URL (ngrok/tunnel) for the gateway.

**Phase 3 — left unchecked**

- "An x402 flow completes end-to-end against test-mode settlement" — the full chain
  (402 challenge -> proof -> awaiting_settlement -> webhook -> settled) is verified in
  `test/protocol-adapters.test.ts` against real Postgres and real Redis, but with the
  Razorpay SDK replaced by a stand-in. Against the LIVE gateway the flow reaches the
  settlement call and stops at Razorpay's `401 Authentication failed`, because `.env`
  still holds placeholder `rzp_test_xxxx` keys. Fill in real test-mode keys and run
  `npm run demo:protocols --workspace=gateway` to close this one.

**Phase 3 — decisions**

- `PolicyEngine.evaluate()` is called by a shared pipeline (`pipeline/payment-pipeline.ts`)
  rather than by each adapter individually. Same guarantee, enforced structurally:
  `settle()` is only reachable through the approved branch of that pipeline, so a future
  adapter cannot forget to check policy. This IS a deviation from the literal "every
  adapter must call PolicyEngine.evaluate()" instruction.
- x402's one-time `reference` reuses the AP2 nonce guard rather than a second mechanism,
  per §3.2's final bullet — the two are the same kind of single-use token.
- The fallback adapter settles via Payment Link and has no `razorpay_orders` row, so the
  webhook route now also resolves a payment_request from `notes.paymentRequestId`.
  Without that the fallback path could never reach 'settled'.
- §3.3's `deriveIdempotencyKey` was implemented here (nominally Phase 2) because
  `payment_requests.idempotency_key` is NOT NULL UNIQUE — no adapter can persist a
  request without it.

**Phase 2 — decisions**

- §3.5's pseudo-SQL says "ROLLBACK" on a spend-cap breach, but §3.5 step 4 requires the
  rejection be written to `audit_log`. A rollback would discard that audit row, so a
  breach COMMITS the audit row and leaves `spent_paise` untouched — identical to a
  rollback for the money, and the only way to satisfy both steps atomically.
